e": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary": "Plan-stage inspection, approach, assumptions", "stage": "plan", "answer": "# Initial Inspection & Approach Sketch (plan stage)\n\n## 1) Repository inspection \u2014 what is actually here\n\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json`. No sub-directories.\n- `task.json.documents` contains a single entry, `track.md`, whose body reads: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"* (Re-confirmed by `find /workspace -type f`.)\n- No source files, README, configs, benchmark scripts, or model code anywhere under `/workspace` (verified with `find /workspace -type f`). A wider search (`find / -maxdepth 5 -name \"*.py\"`, filtered to non-system paths) returns only system scripts (`/bin/mesa-overlay-control.py`); nothing that references 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-H1 / ZB-H2 (a.k.a. ZB-1p / ZB-2p), or zero-bubble pipeline parallelism.\n- Environment metadata says `gpu_enabled: false`, `network: disabled`, and \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\" So no real run is expected, and even a successful shell command is not evidence of a completed experiment.\n- **Consequence for the design:** every claim about \"what the implementation does\" has to come from the public methods (PipeDream / PipeDream-Flush / \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\", Qi et al. ICLR 2024), not from this repo. Any experimental design must therefore be (a) a *theoretical / schedule-analytic* design that the report itself makes self-evident, or (b) a *concrete deferred-run spec* that names where the four schedules will live at run time. I propose (a) as the primary deliverable and (b) as a clearly-marked appendix contingent on the researcher pointing at an implementation source.\n\n## 2) Proposed approach (sketch only \u2014 not the final design)\n\n**Goal of the experiment.** For each schedule `S \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p (ZB-H1), ZB-2p (ZB-H2)}`, measure steady-state throughput as a function of the number of microbatches `M`, holding everything else fixed, and check whether the curves match the predicted shape:\n\n| Schedule | Predicted throughput vs M (theory)                                                                                 |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 1F1B     | Rises with M, bubble fraction `(P\u22121)/(P\u22121+M)` \u2192 asymptote 1, slow when M \u2272 P                                       |\n| 1F1B-I   | Same shape as 1F1B but with effective depth `P\u00b7V` and effective M `M\u00b7V`; needs `M\u00b7V \u2265 P\u00b7V` to enter steady state    |\n| ZB-1p    | Rises with M (smaller bubble than 1F1B, non-zero)                                                                  |\n| ZB-2p    | Roughly **flat** in M; bubble \u2248 0 by construction; any residual dependence is only from launch / comms overhead |\n\nThe \"few vs many\" claim becomes visible if we span M from just above `P` (few) to `M \u226b P` (many).\n\n**Independent variables (sweeps).**\n- `M` (microbatches) \u2014 primary axis. Suggested grid: `{P, 2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P}` (and optionally `64P`).\n- `S` \u2014 the four schedules, plus a \"no-PP / ideal\" upper-bound curve (memory-free, bubble-free).\n- Optional secondary sweep: `P \u2208 {4, 8, 16}` to confirm the predicted `P`-dependence of the gap.\n\n**Dependent variable (the metric).**\n- Primary: **steady-state tokens / second / device**, computed as `M\u00b7\u00b5\u00b7L / wall_time_per_global_step`, recorded by the `step_time` field of each schedule, after discarding the first `P\u22121`-microbatch warmup (and any 1F1B warmup phase). All four methods count only post-warmup, post-cooldown steps.\n- Secondary: model FLOPs utilization (MFU) = achieved TFLOPs/s \u00f7 peak TFLOPs/s of the assumed GPU. MFU is GPU-agnostic and is the canonical \"throughput that doesn't lie about hardware differences\" in the pipeline-parallelism literature.\n- Report mean \u00b1 std over `R \u2265 3` repeats (different random seeds) for each `(S, M)`.\n\n**Controls / fairness invariants (must be the same across `S` at fixed `M`).**\n- Same FP precision (BF16 params / FP32 master, matching the ZB paper).\n- Same microbatch size `\u00b5`; vary `M` so total global batch = `M\u00b7\u00b5` grows *with M*. (Alternative: hold global batch constant and shrink `\u00b5`. Cleaner for the bubble theory is \"fix `\u00b5`, grow M\" \u2014 that isolates the bubble term \u2014 but the user's framing about \"few microbatches\" more naturally fits a fixed-total-budget setup. I will default to fix-`\u00b5` and call out the alternative for the researcher to pick.)\n- Same model (architecture, depth, width, #params, sequence length `L`).\n- Same pipeline depth `P`, same tensor-parallel and data-parallel degrees, same optimizer, same gradient clipping rule.\n- Same number of warmup and measured steps.\n- Same communication backend for PP peer-to-peer sends/recvs (so the four schedules aren't accidentally differentiated by NCCL choice).\n- Activation recomputation: full vs. selective vs. none \u2014 pick one and hold constant across `S` and `M`. (Full recomputation changes effective M costs, so this is a meaningful lever; it should be a controlled fixed setting, not a confounder.)\n- 1F1B-I: fix the number of virtual stages `V` (canonical `V = 2`; do not also vary `V`, since `V` is itself a confounding knob).\n\n**Plot shape.** One figure, `M` on the x-axis (log or linear \u2014 log is more readable because the theoretical `M/(M+P\u22121)` curve is widely-spaced at small M and saturates at large M), one line per schedule with shaded `\u00b11\u03c3` bands. Annotate the theoretical bubble ratio for 1F1B at each `M` as dashed reference lines. A second figure shows the *gap-to-ZB-2p* `T_{S}(M) \u2212 T_{ZB-2p}(M)`; the central claim (ZB-2p stays flat, the others rise and only catch up at very large M) is visible as three