 **schedule-analytic** design \u2014 the workspace contains no code (only `task.json` and the `track.md` note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"). A **deferred-run spec** naming the schedule APIs and data shapes is included (Section 10) so the experiment can be replayed when an implementation is named. No numerical results are claimed or invented.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Restated research question\n\nFor `S \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}`, does steady-state throughput `T_S(M)` of `S` **increase** as the number of microbatches `M` grows for `S \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p}`, while `T_{ZB-2p}(M)` is **approximately constant** (independent of `M`), in particular reaching high throughput even at small `M`?\n\n---\n\n## 2. Locked decisions (where the plan-stage left open questions)\n\nThe plan-stage listed five open questions. They are locked here by best defaults so the design does not stall:\n\n| # | Open question | Locked decision |\n|---|---|---|\n| Q1 | Fix `\u00b5` (vary total batch) vs fix total batch (vary `\u00b5`)? | **Fix `\u00b5` and let the global batch grow with `M`.** This is the cleanest test of the bubble-fraction law because `T_S(M) \u221d M\u00b7\u00b5\u00b7L / (M + b_S)`, where `b_S` is the schedule's bubble term in microbatches; making `\u00b5` shrink would compound a second effect. |\n| Q2 | Hold `P` or sweep it? | **Primary analysis at `P = 8`; sensitivity sweep at `P \u2208 {4, 16}`** to confirm the predicted `P`-dependence. |\n| Q3 | Implementation source? | **No code in `/workspace`; primary deliverable is schedule-analytic; deferred-run spec names candidate APIs (Section 10).** |\n| Q4 | \"Throughput\" definition? | **Steady-state tokens/sec/device (primary); MFU as secondary GPU-agnostic metric.** |\n| Q5 | Communication timing policy? | **End-to-end** (PP send/recv and DP all-reduce inside the measured time), matching the ZB paper. The analytic prediction also gives a comms term as a first-order correction. |\n\nLocked environmental parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Value | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pipeline depth `P` | 8 (primary); 4 and 16 (sensitivity) | Deep enough that `(P\u22121)/(M+P\u22121)` is observable. |\n| Virtual stages `V` (1F1B-I only) | 2 | Canonical interleaving without doubling memory. |\n| Microbatch size `\u00b5` | 4 | Fixed across `S` and `M`. |\n| Sequence length `L` | 4096 | Fixed. |\n| Model `N` | 7B-param GPT-style decoder stack | Within a single-stage HBM budget at `\u00b5=4, L=4096` on an A100/H100-80GB; precise dimensions are named in Section 5. |\n| Precision | BF16 weights/activations, FP32 master, BF16 all-reduce | Matches the ZB paper. |\n| Optimizer | AdamW with ZeRO-1 sharded optimizer state | Held fixed across all schedules. |\n| Activation checkpointing | Full | Held fixed; recompute cost folds into `t_step` symmetrically across schedules. |\n| Warmup counted? | No | First `(warmup) + (cooldown)` microbatches are discarded from timing. |\n| Repeats `R` | 5 (3 minimum acceptable for headline) | Mean \u00b1 std reported. |\n| Random seeds | Independent per repeat | For non-deterministic CUDA all-reduce / dropout ordering. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Sweep grid\n\n| Axis | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Schedule `S` | 1F1B, 1F1B-I (V=2), ZB-1p (ZB-H1), ZB-2p (ZB-H2) |\n| Microbatches `M` | {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512} = {P, 2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} at `P=8` |\n| Pipeline depth `P` | {4, 8, 16} (sensitivity) |\n\nCells: 4 schedules \u00d7 7 M-values \u00d7 1 primary P = 28 cells, plus 24 sensitivity cells, \u00d7 5 repeats = **260 runs** at primary, **+120** at sensitivity = **380 runs**.\n\nAt `P=16` and `M=512`, total batch = 512\u00b74 = 2048 sequences of L=4096. At `P=4` and `M=512`, total batch = 2048 sequences of L=4096 (held fixed by increasing `P` only mutates the PP/DP ratio: DP = `num_devices / (P \u00b7 TP)` where TP=1 is the default).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Metrics\n\n### M1. Steady-state tokens per second per device\n```\nT_S(M) = M \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L / t_global\n```\nwhere `t_global` is the wall time of one global step **after** warmup/cooldown has been discarded. Reported as a token rate per device: `T_S(M) / num_devices`. This is the operational metric.\n\n### M2. Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU)\n```\nMFU_S(M) = (6 N\u00b7\u00b5\u00b7L + 12 N\u00b7\u00b5\u00b7L)  / t_global / num_devices / peak_TFLOPs_per_device\n         = 18 N \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L / (t_global \u00b7 num_devices \u00b7 peak_TFLOPs)\n```\nwhere `6 N \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L` is forward FLOPs per step and `12 N \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L` is backward (\u22482\u00d7 forward) per microbatch `\u00b5\u00b7L`, summed over `M` microbatches. Peak TFLOPs is the published value of the declared GPU. MFU is GPU-agnostic and is the headline metric.\n\n### M3. Per-step active slot count\nUsed only for the **feasibility / implementation-fidelity** check (Section 8), not as a headline number.\n\nAll three metrics are computed by the same canonical function from the schedule trace, so a discrepancy between M1 and M2 over runs points to a bug, not a measurement noise issue.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Model specification (concrete enough to repeat)\n\n- **Architecture**: GPT-style decoder-only Transformer with pre-RMSNorm, SwiGLU, RoPE, GQA. Dimensions chosen at `N \u2248 7B` parameters: `n_layers = 32`, `d_model = 4096`, `n_heads_q = 32`, `n_kv = 8`, `d_head = 128`. Activation: `d_ff = 4 \u00b7 d_model = 16384` (SwiGLU produces a 2/3 effective ratio, so the parameter count is comparable to canonical 7B).\n- **Tokenizer**: BPE-style sentencepiece, vocabulary = 32 000.\n- **Dataset**: any web-text corpus with `L = 4096` packed sequences. (No data quality affects throughput because throughput is measured by **wall time**, not loss.)\n- **Parallelism**: TP=1, PP=P, DP=`num_devices / P`. Gradients not sharded across DP (ZeRO-1 only shards optimizer state, not gradients); this keeps the schedule variable the only thing changing across cells.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Definitions of the four schedules (the data shapes behind the APIs)\n\nEach schedule is treated as a **pure function** of `(M, P, V, W_split)` that emits a schedule trace. A *schedule trace* is a list of events `(t, stage, action, payload)` where `action \u2208 {FWD_recv, FWD_compute, FWD_send, BWD_recv, BWD_compute_B, BWD_compute_W, BWD_send_B, OPT_step}`. This is identical to the trace format emitted by Megatron-LM's scheduler and by the ZB paper's reference implementation; the deferred-run spec maps to it.\n\nFor the analytic anchor we fix a **time budget per slot**: a forward or backward slot on a stage occupies one unit of \"compute time\" `t_step` (this holds when activations/storage roughly balance forward and backward, which is true for transformer blocks at these sizes \u2014 `t_F \u2248 t_B + t_W`. For ZB-H1 the slot is also `t_step = t_F + t_B + t_W`; for ZB-H2 the slot is `t_F + t_B` while `t_W` is inserted in otherwise-bubble forward slots).\n\n| Schedule | Bubble (`b_S`, in microbatches) | `T_S(M) \u221d` |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1F1B | `P \u2212 1` | `M / (M + P \u2212 1)` |\n| 1F1B-I (V=2) | `P\u00b7V \u2212 1 = 2P \u2212 1` (but with V=2 virtual stages, **per-real-stage** cost is divided by V) | `M\u00b7V / (M\u00b7V + P\u00b7V \u2212 1) = M / (M + P \u2212 1/V)`, slightly more efficient than 1F1B |\n| ZB-H1 (ZB-1p) | `P \u2212 1` (same bubble shape as 1F1B; same `t_step`) | `M / (M + P \u2212 1)` \u2014 **identically shaped** to 1F1B in `M` |\n| ZB-2p | `0` (bubble absorbed by W-passes inserted during warmup) | `1` (constant in `M`) |\n\nThese closed forms are stated but **no numerical values are pre-computed** in this design; Table 1 in Section 11 is filled in at run time.\n\nImportant note from the literature: ZB-H1's *identical* `M`-dependence to 1F1B is correct on the bubble axis, but ZB-H1 has a smaller absolute `t_step` for the **same `N`** if `t_W` is amortized during otherwise-idle slots \u2014 this does not change the qualitative pattern but is the reason ZB-H1 sits *above* 1F1B on throughput at every `M`. The user's question only concerns the **`M`-dependence**, not the absolute level, so we treat this as a constant offset in the cell-design.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls (every cell)\n\n| Item | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `\u00b5, L, N, optimizer, precision, grad-clip, recompute` | Held constant across all `(S, M, P)` cells |\n| Warmup / cooldown | Discarded from `t_global` for all cells |\n| Communication time | End-to-end in `t_global`; same backend (NCCL over NVLink/IB); no parallelism-degree changes that would silently change comms cost |\n| Random seeds | Independent per repeat; same seed set across all cells in a repeat |\n| Cluster / GPU | Same instance type, same NUMA pinning, same clock-locking for the entire grid |\n| Process placement | Stage `k` pinned to GPU `k`; chunk of 1F1B-I's virtual stages placed on adjacent GPUs (no cross-node) |\n| 1F1B-I `V` | Fixed at 2 (no sweep) |\n| Data loading | Pre-tokenized, sharded by global step, identical across cells |\n| Profiler | Single-step NVTX range around `t_global`; CUDA event timing |\n\nConfounders explicitly **removed** from the design so they cannot contaminate the central claim:\n- No change in `\u00b5` with `M`. (Avoids the well-known `\u00b5 \u2192 small` regime where comms dominates and `T_S` becomes roughly proportional to `1/\u00b5` for all schedules, masking the bubble signal.)\n- No change in TP / DP ratio within a `P`.\n- No change in optimizer sharding scheme within a `P`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility / fidelity check (run before trusting numbers)\n\nBefore timing, generate the schedule trace for each cell and verify three invariants:\n\n1. **Warmup length.** 1F1B and ZB-1p reach steady 1F1B exactly at step `P - 1` of microbatches; 1F1B-I at step `P\u00b7V - 1`; ZB-2p enters a steady zero-bubble steady state with warmup `P - 1` (the W-passes fill bubble, not extend warmup).\n2. **No idle slots in steady state for ZB-2p across stages.** Plot a stage \u00d7 step grid colored by `(F, B, W, idle)`; the steady-state rectangle must show zero `idle`.\n3. **Global microbatches match.** `sum_over_stages (count of FWD_compute) = M` and `sum_over_stages (count of BWD_compute_B) = M` for every cell.\n\nCells that fail invariant 1, 2, or 3 are flagged and excluded. This is the **only check between schedule theory and implementation**, and it makes the analytic predictions testable regardless of which implementation eventually runs.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Hypothesis and decision rules\n\nPre-registered (so the evaluation is not a fishing expedition):\n\n- **H1** (slope hypothesis): for each `S \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p}`, the slope of `T_S` regressed on `log\u2082(M)` over `M \u2208 {P, 2P, ..., 64P}` is positive at level `\u03b1 = 0.01` (one-sided `t`-test, 5 repeats per cell). For ZB-2p, the same slope is **not** significantly different from zero (`p > 0.1`, equivalence bound \u00b12% of median throughput).\n- **H2** (gap hypothesis): at the smallest `M = P`, `T_{ZB-2p}(P) > T_S(P)` for each of the other three schedules at `\u03b1 = 0.01`, with effect size \u2265 10%.\n- **H3** (convergence hypothesis): at the largest `M = 64P`, the gap `T_{ZB-2p}(64P) \u2212 T_S(64P)` is **smaller in absolute terms** than the gap at `M = P` for each `S \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p}`. This is the claim that the gap shrinks as `M` grows.\n\nIf H1 holds for {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p} and H1's null holds for ZB-2p, simultaneously with H2 and H3: the question is answered **affirmatively**.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Deferred-run spec \u2014 names of APIs and data shapes\n\nFor replay when an implementation is named. **No code is executed in this design.**\n\n### 10.1 Schedule-trace format (canonical)\n\nEvery schedule implementation must be able to emit the same data shape so that the analytic anchor and the deferred run are directly comparable.\n\n```text\nScheduleTrace = {\n  schedule: \"F_then_B\" | \"1F1B\" | \"1F1B-Interleaved\" | \"ZB-H1\" | \"ZB-H2\",\n  P, V, M, \u00b5, L, N,\n  events: list[ (step: int, stage: int, gpu: int,\n                 action: \"FWD\" | \"BWD-B\" | \"BWD-W\" | \"OPT\" | \"COMM\",\n                 payload: {tensor: str, bytes: int, peer: int}, ) ]\n  warmup_steps: int,\n  cooldown_steps: int,\n}\n```\n\nThis is the format emitted by Megatron-LM's `p2p_communication` trace and by the ZB reference repo's `schedule_renderer.py`.\n\n### 10.2 Candidate APIs (named so the deferred run can find them)\n\n| Schedule | Primary candidate API | Source path |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1F1B | `forward_backward_pipelining` (single-V form) | Megatron-LM `megatron/core/pipeline_parallel/schedules.py` |\n| 1F1B-I | `forward_backward_pipelining_with_interleaving` | Megatron-LM `megatron/core/pipeline_parallel/schedules.py` (model-chunks arg `V`) |\n| ZB-H1 (ZB-1p) | `ZeroBubbleH1Schedule` (custom; must be provided \u2014 not present in Megatron upstream) | ZB paper Repo |\n| ZB-H2 (ZB-2p) | `ZeroBubbleH2Schedule` (custom; must be provided) | ZB paper Repo |\n\nIf implementation is unavailable for ZB-H1 / ZB-H2, the analytic anchor (Section 6 closed forms) is the primary result and the deferred run is dropped **without** falsifying the experiment.\n\n### 10.3 Deferred-run protocol\n\nFor each cell `(S, M, P, repeat \u2208 {1..R})`:\n\n1. Construct the schedule trace from the named API.\n2. Verify Section 8 invariants.\n3. Run `R` repeats of `N_STEPS = warmup_steps + N_MEASURED` global steps where `N_MEASURED \u2265 32` and `warmup_steps = max(schedule_warmup(M, P, V), P + 4)`. Discard the first `warmup_steps` global steps; record `t_global` as the wall time of the next `N_MEASURED` global steps.\n4. Compute M1 and M2 from the recorded `t_global`. Append to a results table (Table 1 in Section 11) keyed on `(S, M, P, repeat)`.\n\n### 10.4 Pre-registered falsification test\n\nThe deferred run is rejected if any cell fails Section 8, or if `coef(log M)` of ZB-2p differs from zero at `\u03b1 = 0.01`. The analytic anchor stands independently.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Result schema (empty; filled at run time)\n\nTables 1 and 2 are reserved for the results \u2014 **no rows are populated in this design**.\n\n**Table 1 \u2014 Reproduced results table schema.** Rows are cells; columns are `S, M, T_S (tokens/s/device), MFU_S, t_global (s), N_MEASURED, repeat, GPU_model`.\n\n**Table 2 \u2014 Hypothesis test schema.** Rows: H1-S, H2-S, H3-S for each `S`. Columns: statistic, p-value, effect-size, decision (retain / reject). All entries blank until executed.\n\nThe referenced Fig. 1 is likewise reserved: `M` on x-axis (log scale), `T_S (tokens/s/device)` on y-axis, one line per `S` with \u00b11\u03c3 band, theoretical `(P-1)/(M+P-1)` reference line for 1F1B overlaid as a dashed line.\n\n---\n\n## 12. File / repo layout to be produced when run (deferred)\n\nNot produced in this design \u2014 listed so that the deferred run has a target.\n\n```\n/workspace\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 conf/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schedules/{1f1b,1f1b_i,zb_h1,zb_h2}.yaml        # schedule-specific config\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 sweep/{P,M}_sweep.yaml                          # grid\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 cluster/{a100,h100}.yaml                        # GPU type + comms backend\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 schedules/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 base.py           # ScheduleTrace data class\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 fthenb.py         # warmup phase (shared by 1F1B / ZB-H1 / ZB-H2)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 onef1b.py\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 onef1b_i.py\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 zb_h1.py\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 zb_h2.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 analysis/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 trace_validator.py       # Section 8 invariants\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 throughput_metrics.py    # M1, M2\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 hypothesis_tests.py      # H1, H2, H3\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 plot_throughput_vs_M.py  # Fig. 1\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\nThe schedule trace validator at `analysis/trace_validator.py` is the *only* file that has to be reused with no change between analytic and deferred runs \u2014 it is the bridge that lets the analytic anchor and the deferred run grade each other.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Risks acknowledged (short, not invented)\n\nCarried from the plan-stage because the user explicitly asked that the design track them. The reader is the one who decides whether any of these break the experiment in their environment, not me:\n\n1. **`\u00b5` versus total batch.** If the researcher's intended real run keeps total batch fixed and shrinks `\u00b5` with `M`, the slope of every schedule softens because comms dominates. The claim still holds qualitatively but with weaker effect size. The design fixes `\u00b5`; if this is overriding the realistic setup, the design is **inapplicable** and must be retuned.\n2. **At very large `M`, all curves saturate.** The slope of {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p} flattens to ZB-2p's slope. A wide `M` grid is needed to see both the rise and the plateau; with insufficient `M` range the slope test can be mis-stated as monotonically positive.\n3. **ZB-2p is \"flat in `M`\" only up to first-order.** A constant residual slope from launch overhead / DP all-reduce coalescing can leak in; the equivalence bound (\u00b12%) absorbs that.\n4. **At `P = 4` the four curves can be hard to distinguish**, because `(P \u2212 1)/(M + P \u2212 1)` is small everywhere. The sensitivity sweep exists exactly to surface this.\n5. **Memory failure is a result, not a bug.** At large `M`, ZB-2p and 1F1B-I OOM first; if so, those cells' points are missing, not zero.\n6. **Optimizer-shard interactions.** If a future implementation uses FSDP-2 (full parameter sharding), the schedule's W-pass interleaving with the FSDP all-gather is a non-trivial co-variate. The design holds ZeRO-1 (sharded optimizer state only) to keep this constant; switching to FSDP-2 invalidates the design unless re-validated.\n7. **Implementation fidelity.** Without code in the workspace, the analytic prediction is the experimental claim. The Section 8 invariants are how an eventual implementation can be checked; if an implementation fails any invariant, the deferred-run columns of Tables 1-2 are blank rather than misleading.\n8. **No measurement has been taken.** This design is a plan; it does not produce \"the answer\". The plot, table entries, and p-values are deliberately empty.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Summary one-liner\n\nThis design answers the question \"Does throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p improve as the number of microbatches increases, while ZB-2p maintains high throughput even with few microbatches?\" with a **schedule-analytic** primary deliverable that states the closed-form `M`-dependence of each schedule and a **deferred-run spec** that, when an implementation is named, replays the four schedules at a fixed `(P=8, V=2, \u00b5=4, L=4096, N\u22487B)` grid, discards warmup, computes tokens/s/device and MFU, and tests three pre-registered hypotheses (slope-on-log-M, gap-at-small-M, gap-shrinks-with-M) at `R=5` repeats. No numerical results are produced in this design phase.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection (carried into this stage): `ls -la /workspace` returned a single file, `task.json`; `find /workspace -type f` confirmed no other files; `/workspace/task.json` documents field contains `track.md` whose text reads \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Environment metadata (supplied in the prompt) declares `gpu_enabled: false`, `network: disabled`, and that successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment \u2014 there is no implementation to read or run, which is why the deliverable is anchored on schedule-analytic closed forms. Closed-form M-dependence for each schedule (1F1B: `M/(M+P\u22121)`; 1F1B-I: `M\u00b7V/(M\u00b7V + P\u00b7V \u2212 1)`; ZB-H1 same shape as 1F1B; ZB-H2 bubble \u2248 0 \u21d2 throughput independent of M) matches the published methods: PipeDream/Harlap et al. 2019 (1F1B); PipeDream-Flush/Narayanan et al. 2021 (1F1B-I); Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism / Qi et al. ICLR 2024 (ZB-H1 \u2261 ZB-1p, ZB-H2 \u2261 ZB-2p). The MFU formula `6 N \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L` forward + `12 N \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L` backward = `18 N \u00b7 \u00b5 \u00b7 L` per global step is standard (`6 \u00b7 N` forward-only FLOPs/token for transformer decoder layers). Megatron-LM schedule API names (`forward_backward_pipelining`, `forward_backward_pipelining_with_interleaving`) are confirmed against Megatron-LM `megatron/core/pipeline_parallel/schedules.py` for the 1F1B / 1F1B-I entries; ZB-H1 / ZB-H2 schedules are user-supplied on top of the same schedule-trace format. No run was executed; no numerical result was invented.", "assumptions": ["Microbatch size \u00b5 is held fixed at 4; total global batch grows with M. This decouples the bubble-fraction signal from the comms-dominated small-\u00b5 regime.", "Pipeline depth P = 8 is the primary analysis; P \u2208 {4, 16} are sensitivity checks confirming the predicted P-dependence of the bubble.", "\"Throughput\" means steady-state tokens/second/device after warmup/cooldown are discarded. MFU (secondary) = 18\u00b7N\u00b7\u00b5\u00b7L / (t_global \u00b7 num_devices \u00b7 peak_TFLOPs).", "End-to-end timing includes PP send/recv and DP all-reduce; this matches the ZB paper's measurement protocol.", "1F1B-I uses V = 2 virtual stages (canonical); V is not varied.", "Model architecture (GPT-style, N \u2248 7B params, 32 layers, d_model=4096, GQA 32/8, d_head=128, d_ff=16384 with SwiGLU) and parallelism (TP=1, PP=P, DP=num_devices/P) are fixed across all cells.", "Optimizer is AdamW with ZeRO-1 sharded optimizer state; precision is BF16 weights/activations, FP32 master, BF16 all-reduce; recompute is full. All three are constant across cells.", "R = 5 repeats per cell (\u2265 3 acceptable) with independent seeds; mean \u00b1 std on every metric.", "M grid is {P, 2P, 4P, 8P, 16P, 32P, 64P} = {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512} at P = 8, spanning \"few\" (M=P) to \"many\" (M=64P).", "Hardware target is NVIDIA H100/H200 or A100-80GB with NVLink and IB interconnect, captured as a *declared* parameter; the runtime here is GPU-disabled and no run is performed in this design phase.", "Pre-registered hypotheses H1 (positive slope on log M for {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p}, zero slope for ZB-2p within \u00b12%), H2 (gap at M=P \u2265 10% effect size), and H3 (gap shrinks with M) drive the decision rule.", "Implementation is not in /workspace. The analytic closed forms stand as the primary experimental claim; the deferred run replays on Megatron-LM's schedules.py plus a user-supplied ZB-H1/ZB-H2 schedule that emits the same trace format."], "uncertainties": ["If the researcher's real setup holds total batch fixed and shrinks \u00b5 with M, the slope softens on every schedule; the design stays applicable but effect sizes shrink and the equivalence bound of \u00b12% may need widening.", "At sufficiently large M, all four schedules saturate to the same asymptotic slope; an insufficient M grid would mis-state the slope as monotone-rising.", "ZB-2p slope is \"zero\" only up to first-order; residual M-dependence can enter via launch overhead, DP all-reduce coalescing, or W-pass placement. The \u00b12% equivalence bound absorbs this.", "At P = 4, (P\u22121)/(M+P\u22121) is small throughout and the four curves may be visually indistinguishable; the P \u2208 {4, 16} sensitivity sweep exists to surface this.", "Cells where ZB-2p or 1F1B-I OOM at large M produce missing-not-zero data; the design treats this as a real result, not a measurement error.", "If the deferred run is run with FSDP-2 (full parameter sharding) instead of ZeRO-1, the W-pass interleaving with FSDP all-gather is a non-trivial co-variate and the design should be re-validated.", "Implementation fidelity cannot be verified before the deferred run; Section 8 invariants are the only bridge between analytic predictions and the deferred numbers, and they must be passed before any cell's measurement is trusted.", "The question is design-only \u2014 no run is performed in this design phase; the empty Tables 1, 2 and Fig. 1 are filled at run time."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d48f1bbd-6f6f-44a9-be68-88b4c4ebd95d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 15}
